    "q-109743": {
      "dom_id": "step-q109743",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Suppose that a matrix $C$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{4}(\\lambda -\\pi )^{2}(\\lambda +11)^{7},$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0,$ there is exactly $1$ eigenvector. If a basis of ${R}^{13}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $C$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0?$",
      "worked_solution": "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{4}(\\lambda -\\pi )^{2}(\\lambda +11)^{7},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =0$ has multiplicity $4.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ is $4.$\n\nHowever, there is only $1$ eigenvector corresponding to  the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ is $1.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =0$ is $4-1=3.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
