      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Number of Generalized Eigenvectors of Rank Greater Than One",
      "kp_id": "e-9055"
    },
    "q-109741": {
      "dom_id": "step-q109741",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Suppose that a matrix $S$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(e-\\lambda )^{5}(\\lambda +\\sqrt{3})^{4}(5-\\lambda )^{7},$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda =e,$ there are exactly $2$ eigenvectors. If a basis of ${R}^{16}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $S,$ how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =e?$",
      "worked_solution": "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )=(e-\\lambda )^{5}(\\lambda +\\sqrt{3})^{4}(5-\\lambda )^{7},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =e$ has multiplicity $5.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =e$ is $5.$\n\nHowever, there are only $2$ eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =e,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =e$ is $2.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =e$ is $5-2=3.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
