    "q-109752": {
      "dom_id": "step-q109752",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Suppose that a matrix $C$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(3-\\lambda )^{2}(\\lambda -\\sqrt{2})^{15}(\\lambda -3)^{4},$ the nullity of $(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{2}$ equals $2,$ and the nullity of $(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{3}$ equals $4.$ If a basis of ${R}^{21}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $C$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "For the given matrix $C$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{2},$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{2}$ has dimension $2.$ This means that the solutions to $(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{2}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $2.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{3}$ has dimension $4.$ This means that the solutions to $(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $4.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{2}$ is a subspace of dimension $2$ in $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{3}$, which is a subspace of dimension $4,$ our basis can contain only $2$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{3}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{3}-\\text{nullity}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{2}=4-2=2\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{2}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
