    "q-109840": {
      "dom_id": "step-q109840",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Suppose that a matrix $S$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(7+\\lambda )^{3}(\\lambda -\\sqrt{3})^{4}(\\lambda +2)^{3},$ the nullity of $(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{3}$ equals $3,$ and the nullity of $(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{4}$ equals $4.$ If a basis of ${R}^{10}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $S,$ how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{3}?$",
      "worked_solution": "For the given matrix $S$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{3},$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{4}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{3}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{3}$ has dimension $3.$ This means that the solutions to $(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $3.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{4}$ has dimension $4.$ This means that the solutions to $(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{4}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $4.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{3}$ is a subspace of dimension $3$ in $\\text{Null}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{4}$, which is a subspace of dimension $4,$ our basis can contain only $1$ vector that lies in $\\text{Null}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{4}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{4}-\\text{nullity}(S-\\sqrt{3}I)^{3}=4-3=1\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{3}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
