    "q-109746": {
      "dom_id": "step-q109746",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(\\lambda +1)^{2}(\\lambda -3)^{6}(\\lambda +2)^{3},$ the nullity of $(A-3I)^{3}$ equals $3,$ and the nullity of $(A-3I)^{4}$ equals $4.$ If a basis of ${R}^{11}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3?$",
      "worked_solution": "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =3,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(A-3I)^{4}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-3I)^{3}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A-3I)^{3}$ has dimension $3.$ This means that the solutions to $(A-3I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $3.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A-3I)^{4}$ has dimension $4.$ This means that the solutions to $(A-3I)^{4}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $4.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(A-3I)^{3}$ is a subspace of dimension $3$ in $\\text{Null}(A-3I)^{4}$, which is a subspace of dimension $4,$ our basis can contain only $1$ vector that lies in $\\text{Null}(A-3I)^{4}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(A-3I)^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-3I)^{4}-\\text{nullity}(A-3I)^{3}=4-3=1\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
