    },
    "q-78160": {
      "dom_id": "step-q78160",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=\\lambda (\\lambda +1)^{3}(\\lambda -2)^{4},$ the rank of $(A-2I)^{2}$ equals $7,$ and the rank of $(A-2I)^{3}$ equals $6.$ If a basis of ${R}^{8}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2?$",
      "worked_solution": "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(A-2I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-2I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ must be an $8\\times 8$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-2I)^{2}$ equals $7,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-2I)^{2}=8-7=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A-2I)^{2}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{2}$ of dimension $1$ in ${R}^{8}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-2I)^{3}$ equals $6,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-2I)^{3}=8-6=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A-2I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{3}$ of dimension $2$ in ${R}^{8}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{2}$ is a subspace of dimension $1$ in $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{3}$, which is a subspace of dimension $2,$ our basis can contain only $1$ vector that lies in $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{3}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(A-2I)^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-2I)^{3}-\\text{nullity}(A-2I)^{2}=2-1=1\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
