    "q-78478": {
      "dom_id": "step-q78478",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(\\lambda +1)^{2}(\\lambda -7)^{12},$ the rank of $(A-7I)^{5}$ equals $3,$ and the rank of $(A-7I)^{4}$ equals $5.$ If a basis of ${R}^{14}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $5$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7?$",
      "worked_solution": "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =7,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $5$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(A-7I)^{5}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-7I)^{4}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ must be a $14\\times 14$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-7I)^{4}$ equals $5,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-7I)^{4}=14-5=9.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A-7I)^{4}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-7I)^{4}$ of dimension $9$ in ${R}^{14}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-7I)^{5}$ equals $3,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-7I)^{5}=14-3=11.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A-7I)^{5}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-7I)^{5}$ of dimension $11$ in ${R}^{14}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(A-7I)^{4}$ is a subspace of dimension $9$ in $\\text{Null}(A-7I)^{5}$, which is a subspace of dimension $11,$ our basis can contain only $2$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(A-7I)^{5}$ but do not lie in $\\text{Null}(A-7I)^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-7I)^{5}-\\text{nullity}(A-7I)^{4}=11-9=2\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $5$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
